1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:02,225
Type this into a language model:

2
00:00:02,225 --> 00:00:04,950
The Eiffel Tower is located in the city of...

3
00:00:04,950 --> 00:00:07,117
and ask it what comes next.

4
00:00:07,117 --> 00:00:09,417
It won't answer with a single word.

5
00:00:09,417 --> 00:00:13,529
It answers with a probability for every possible next piece of text.

6
00:00:13,529 --> 00:00:19,179
Here's what GPT-2, a real model released by OpenAI in 2019, says.

7
00:00:19,179 --> 00:00:24,533
Paris is its top guess, at about six percent, with London close behind.

8
00:00:24,533 --> 00:00:27,658
Now pick a token, append it to the text, and ask again.

9
00:00:27,658 --> 00:00:28,508
And again.

10
00:00:28,508 --> 00:00:30,858
And again.

11
00:00:33,208 --> 00:00:37,096
Today's AI chatbots write every reply exactly like this:

12
00:00:37,096 --> 00:00:39,046
one small piece at a time.

13
00:00:39,046 --> 00:00:41,921
Predict what comes next, append it, repeat.

14
00:00:41,921 --> 00:00:45,758
That's all a large language model does.

15
00:00:46,408 --> 00:00:48,096
So the real question is:

16
00:00:48,096 --> 00:00:52,083
how does a pile of arithmetic produce such sensible guesses?

17
00:00:52,083 --> 00:00:57,283
The answer is a design called the transformer, introduced in 2017 by researchers

18
00:00:57,283 --> 00:01:01,183
at Google, in a paper titled Attention Is All You Need.

19
00:01:01,183 --> 00:01:03,146
It's the T in GPT:

20
00:01:03,146 --> 00:01:06,067
generative pre-trained transformer.

21
00:01:06,067 --> 00:01:10,454
In this video we'll open the box and follow our sentence through every stage:

22
00:01:10,454 --> 00:01:15,867
how text becomes tokens, tokens become vectors, how attention lets those vectors

23
00:01:15,867 --> 00:01:22,000
share information, what the MLP layers do, and how it all turns into a prediction.

24
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:28,600
Every number you'll see comes from GPT-2 small, a model with 124 million parameters.

25
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:32,987
It's tiny by today's standards, but it's built on the same blueprint as today's

26
00:01:32,987 --> 00:01:34,800
large language models.

27
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:38,750
Then we'll see how it learns, what changes when you make it bigger, and how

28
00:01:38,750 --> 00:01:41,050
a text predictor becomes an assistant.

29
00:01:50,006 --> 00:01:50,869
Step one.

30
00:01:50,869 --> 00:01:53,219
A language model can't read letters.

31
00:01:53,219 --> 00:01:56,939
Text first gets chopped into pieces, called tokens.

32
00:01:56,939 --> 00:02:00,889
Common words, like the, or city, get a single token each.

33
00:02:00,889 --> 00:02:05,027
And notice that the space in front of a word is usually part of its token.

34
00:02:05,027 --> 00:02:09,002
A rarer word, like Eiffel, gets broken into smaller pieces:

35
00:02:09,002 --> 00:02:10,864
E, iff, el.

36
00:02:10,864 --> 00:02:13,252
Each token is really just a number:

37
00:02:13,252 --> 00:02:16,814
its position in a fixed list, called the vocabulary.

38
00:02:16,814 --> 00:02:21,639
GPT-2's vocabulary has 50,257 entries.

39
00:02:21,639 --> 00:02:24,639
From here on, the model never sees letters again;

40
00:02:24,639 --> 00:02:27,489
only these numbers.

41
00:02:27,639 --> 00:02:29,027
Another example.

42
00:02:29,027 --> 00:02:33,477
A long but common word, like unbelievable, gets a token of its own.

43
00:02:33,477 --> 00:02:38,677
But ChatGPT didn't exist when this vocabulary was built, in 2019, so it gets

44
00:02:38,677 --> 00:02:41,789
split into three pieces.

45
00:02:41,939 --> 00:02:43,639
Why chop text up this way?

46
00:02:43,639 --> 00:02:45,602
You could use single characters.

47
00:02:45,602 --> 00:02:49,927
Then the vocabulary is tiny, but every sentence becomes a long sequence, and

48
00:02:49,927 --> 00:02:52,914
the model has to work harder just to see the words.

49
00:02:52,914 --> 00:02:54,877
Or you could use whole words.

50
00:02:54,877 --> 00:02:59,714
Sequences are short, but the vocabulary needs every word, name and typo ever

51
00:02:59,714 --> 00:03:02,502
written, and anything new is a dead end.

52
00:03:02,502 --> 00:03:06,114
Subword tokens are the compromise.

53
00:03:06,248 --> 00:03:11,148
The vocabulary itself is built by an algorithm called byte-pair encoding.

54
00:03:11,148 --> 00:03:15,423
Here it is on a tiny, made-up collection of words, each with a count of how

55
00:03:15,423 --> 00:03:16,973
often it appears.

56
00:03:16,973 --> 00:03:20,656
Start with single characters as the vocabulary.

57
00:03:20,656 --> 00:03:24,706
Now count how often each pair of neighboring symbols appears, weighted by

58
00:03:24,706 --> 00:03:26,669
how common each word is.

59
00:03:26,669 --> 00:03:30,381
The winner is o followed by w, 30 times.

60
00:03:30,381 --> 00:03:34,406
Merge that pair into a new symbol, everywhere it occurs, and add it to the

61
00:03:34,406 --> 00:03:36,206
vocabulary.

62
00:03:36,206 --> 00:03:37,256
Then repeat.

63
00:03:37,256 --> 00:03:42,031
Now l and o w appear together 21 times, so they merge into low.

64
00:03:42,031 --> 00:03:44,181
Next, e and r become e r.

65
00:03:44,181 --> 00:03:47,731
Then tow, tower, and slow.

66
00:03:48,698 --> 00:03:53,898
Frequent chunks, like low, tower, and the ending e r, become single tokens,

67
00:03:53,898 --> 00:03:57,298
while rarer words get spelled out of smaller pieces.

68
00:03:57,298 --> 00:04:03,548
GPT-2's tokenizer starts from the 256 possible bytes and runs 50,000 merges

69
00:04:03,548 --> 00:04:06,410
like this over a large body of text.

70
00:04:06,410 --> 00:04:11,948
Add one special token that marks the end of a document, and you get its 50,257

71
00:04:11,948 --> 00:04:14,273
tokens.

72
00:04:14,406 --> 00:04:17,456
So our sentence has become a list of integers.

73
00:04:17,456 --> 00:04:22,756
But the number 6342 tells the model nothing about what Paris is.

74
00:04:22,756 --> 00:04:26,631
The next step is to give every token a much richer description.

75
00:04:30,842 --> 00:04:35,229
The first thing the model does with each token is look it up in a giant table,

76
00:04:35,229 --> 00:04:37,242
called the embedding matrix.

77
00:04:37,242 --> 00:04:43,554
It has one column for every token in the vocabulary, all 50,257 of them, and

78
00:04:43,554 --> 00:04:47,267
each column holds 768 numbers.

79
00:04:47,267 --> 00:04:53,992
So the token Paris, number 6342, picks out its column, and becomes these 768

80
00:04:53,992 --> 00:04:54,829
numbers:

81
00:04:54,829 --> 00:04:55,917
a vector.

82
00:04:55,917 --> 00:04:58,404
Nobody chooses these numbers by hand.

83
00:04:58,404 --> 00:05:02,367
They start out random and get tuned during training, like every other number

84
00:05:02,367 --> 00:05:04,692
in the model.

85
00:05:04,833 --> 00:05:08,646
Every token in our sentence gets its own vector this way.

86
00:05:08,646 --> 00:05:15,583
I'll draw each one as a column of cells, standing in for its 768 numbers.

87
00:05:15,733 --> 00:05:20,446
A list of two numbers can be drawn as an arrow, or a point, in a plane.

88
00:05:20,446 --> 00:05:27,533
In the same way, a list of 768 numbers is a point in a space with 768 dimensions.

89
00:05:27,533 --> 00:05:31,021
We can't picture that, but we can project it down to two.

90
00:05:31,021 --> 00:05:36,183
Here are GPT-2's embeddings of 35 words, flattened onto the two directions

91
00:05:36,183 --> 00:05:38,350
in which they vary the most.

92
00:05:38,350 --> 00:05:41,462
Nobody told the model which words belong together.

93
00:05:41,462 --> 00:05:46,400
Yet the numbers land together, so do the days of the week, countries, animals,

94
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:47,625
and colors.

95
00:05:47,625 --> 00:05:52,600
Words used in similar ways end up with similar vectors.

96
00:05:52,733 --> 00:05:55,646
Directions in this space can carry meaning too.

97
00:05:55,646 --> 00:05:59,746
Here's a different slice through the same space, chosen to show this.

98
00:05:59,746 --> 00:06:04,208
The step from man to woman is nearly the same as the step from uncle to aunt,

99
00:06:04,208 --> 00:06:06,267
and from king to queen.

100
00:06:06,267 --> 00:06:08,054
So try some arithmetic.

101
00:06:08,054 --> 00:06:11,892
Take the vector for king, subtract man, and add woman.

102
00:06:11,892 --> 00:06:16,167
Then search the whole vocabulary, leaving out the three words we started with,

103
00:06:16,167 --> 00:06:19,904
for the token whose vector points most nearly the same way.

104
00:06:19,904 --> 00:06:24,142
In GPT-2's real embeddings, the winner is queen.

105
00:06:24,142 --> 00:06:29,217
The same trick takes you from Paris, minus France, plus Japan, to Tokyo.

106
00:06:29,217 --> 00:06:33,367
It doesn't work for every pair of words, but it's a striking sign that directions

107
00:06:33,367 --> 00:06:39,467
in this space can encode concepts, like gender, or 'capital city of'.

108
00:06:39,658 --> 00:06:43,508
One operation will matter more than any other for the rest of this video:

109
00:06:43,508 --> 00:06:44,908
the dot product.

110
00:06:44,908 --> 00:06:49,158
Multiply two vectors entry by entry, and add up the results.

111
00:06:49,158 --> 00:06:53,908
Geometrically, it measures how much two vectors point the same way.

112
00:06:53,908 --> 00:06:58,371
It's positive when they're aligned, zero when they're perpendicular, and negative

113
00:06:58,371 --> 00:07:00,821
when they point in opposite directions.

114
00:07:00,821 --> 00:07:05,683
Divide by the two lengths, and you get what's called the cosine similarity.

115
00:07:05,683 --> 00:07:10,608
Measured on GPT-2's embeddings, cat is much more similar to dog and kitten

116
00:07:10,608 --> 00:07:13,121
than to banana, or to democracy.

117
00:07:13,121 --> 00:07:17,783
So tokens are now vectors, and similar tokens point in similar directions.

118
00:07:22,005 --> 00:07:23,430
But there's a problem.

119
00:07:23,430 --> 00:07:28,030
In 'we walked along the river bank', and 'we deposited cash at the bank',

120
00:07:28,030 --> 00:07:32,342
the token bank gets exactly the same vector, because there's only one entry

121
00:07:32,342 --> 00:07:34,238
for it in the table.

122
00:07:34,238 --> 00:07:37,013
Its meaning, though, depends on the words around it:

123
00:07:37,013 --> 00:07:39,638
river in one case, cash in the other.

124
00:07:39,638 --> 00:07:42,425
The same is true of nearly every word.

125
00:07:42,425 --> 00:07:47,113
The vector from the lookup table is only a starting point.

126
00:07:47,263 --> 00:07:49,400
That's what the rest of the network is for.

127
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:51,575
Start with each token's vector.

128
00:07:51,575 --> 00:07:55,925
In a transformer, these vectors flow upward through a stack of layers.

129
00:07:55,925 --> 00:07:59,925
In each layer, there's first an attention step, where the vectors exchange

130
00:07:59,925 --> 00:08:02,196
information with each other.

131
00:08:02,196 --> 00:08:07,221
Then comes a step called an MLP, which processes each vector on its own, without

132
00:08:07,221 --> 00:08:09,038
looking at the others.

133
00:08:09,038 --> 00:08:11,900
Crucially, neither step replaces a vector.

134
00:08:11,900 --> 00:08:16,113
Each one computes a small adjustment, and adds it to the vector that's already

135
00:08:16,113 --> 00:08:16,950
there.

136
00:08:16,950 --> 00:08:20,938
Because of this, the column of vectors running up through the network is called

137
00:08:20,938 --> 00:08:22,563
the residual stream.

138
00:08:22,563 --> 00:08:27,721
Think of it as a shared workspace that every layer reads from and writes to.

139
00:08:27,721 --> 00:08:33,346
GPT-2 small repeats this pair of steps 12 times, each layer with its own learned

140
00:08:33,346 --> 00:08:34,346
weights.

141
00:08:34,346 --> 00:08:38,446
At the very top, the vector at the last position is used to predict the next

142
00:08:38,446 --> 00:08:39,821
token.

143
00:08:39,821 --> 00:08:41,121
One more rule.

144
00:08:41,121 --> 00:08:45,409
When the vectors exchange information, each position may only look at itself

145
00:08:45,409 --> 00:08:48,121
and at positions before it, never after.

146
00:08:48,121 --> 00:08:51,621
We'll see why when we get to training.

147
00:08:51,738 --> 00:08:54,875
We can watch this happen inside GPT-2.

148
00:08:54,875 --> 00:08:58,663
Take the vector at the bank position in each of our two sentences, and measure

149
00:08:58,663 --> 00:09:03,075
how similar they are, using cosine similarity, after each layer.

150
00:09:03,075 --> 00:09:05,738
Before the first layer, they're identical.

151
00:09:05,738 --> 00:09:11,730
Then, layer by layer, they drift apart, as each one absorbs its own context.

152
00:09:11,730 --> 00:09:15,642
For comparison, here's bank in a different sentence that also talks about

153
00:09:15,642 --> 00:09:16,617
a river.

154
00:09:16,617 --> 00:09:19,892
Those two stay almost identical all the way up.

155
00:09:19,892 --> 00:09:23,255
The network has pulled the two meanings of bank apart.

156
00:09:23,255 --> 00:09:26,242
So the heart of a transformer is this mixing step.

157
00:09:26,242 --> 00:09:30,592
How does bank know to pull information from river, and not from walked?

158
00:09:30,592 --> 00:09:32,180
That's the job of attention.

159
00:09:36,452 --> 00:09:40,677
Let's zoom in on a single attention step, from the point of view of the token

160
00:09:40,677 --> 00:09:41,602
bank.

161
00:09:41,602 --> 00:09:43,664
Bank would like to ask a question:

162
00:09:43,664 --> 00:09:48,035
is there anything earlier in the text that tells me what kind of bank I am?

163
00:09:48,035 --> 00:09:52,435
It encodes that question as a vector, called a query, by multiplying its own

164
00:09:52,435 --> 00:09:55,072
vector by a matrix, called W Q.

165
00:09:55,072 --> 00:09:56,647
The query is shorter:

166
00:09:56,647 --> 00:10:00,285
in GPT-2, just 64 numbers.

167
00:10:00,285 --> 00:10:05,047
Meanwhile, every token, including bank itself, produces a key, using a second

168
00:10:05,047 --> 00:10:06,597
matrix, W K.

169
00:10:06,597 --> 00:10:08,822
Think of a key as an advertisement:

170
00:10:08,822 --> 00:10:11,060
here's what I have to offer.

171
00:10:11,060 --> 00:10:15,122
Bank's query is compared with every key, using a dot product.

172
00:10:15,122 --> 00:10:17,635
A higher score means a better match.

173
00:10:17,635 --> 00:10:22,310
These are real numbers, from one of the attention heads in GPT-2's very first

174
00:10:22,310 --> 00:10:25,593
layer, and river has the highest score.

175
00:10:25,593 --> 00:10:30,368
To turn scores into weights, the model uses a function called softmax.

176
00:10:30,368 --> 00:10:34,606
It raises e to the power of each score, which makes everything positive and

177
00:10:34,606 --> 00:10:39,156
exaggerates the differences, then divides by the total, so the weights add

178
00:10:39,156 --> 00:10:40,468
up to one.

179
00:10:40,468 --> 00:10:44,260
Now, sixty percent of bank's attention goes to river.

180
00:10:44,260 --> 00:10:46,547
So what actually gets passed along?

181
00:10:46,547 --> 00:10:51,997
Each token also produces a third vector, its value, using a third matrix,

182
00:10:51,997 --> 00:10:52,635
W V.

183
00:10:52,635 --> 00:10:57,693
The value is the information a token hands over to anyone who attends to it.

184
00:10:57,693 --> 00:11:02,606
Bank's update is a weighted sum of all the values, mostly river's value, plus

185
00:11:02,606 --> 00:11:04,093
a little of the others.

186
00:11:04,093 --> 00:11:08,168
And that update gets added to bank's vector.

187
00:11:08,518 --> 00:11:10,106
In a cartoon picture:

188
00:11:10,106 --> 00:11:14,518
if one direction in the space meant money, and another meant riverside, bank's

189
00:11:14,518 --> 00:11:17,506
starting vector would sit somewhere in between.

190
00:11:17,506 --> 00:11:22,543
The update from attention nudges it toward the riverside meaning.

191
00:11:22,693 --> 00:11:26,781
Every token does this at the same time, each with its own query.

192
00:11:26,781 --> 00:11:30,193
Collect all the weights in a grid, and you get what's called an attention

193
00:11:30,193 --> 00:11:31,231
pattern.

194
00:11:31,231 --> 00:11:35,643
Each row is one token looking back over the text, and each row adds up to

195
00:11:35,643 --> 00:11:36,506
one.

196
00:11:36,506 --> 00:11:39,727
The bottom row is bank, with river lit up.

197
00:11:39,727 --> 00:11:42,164
The gray triangle is off limits:

198
00:11:42,164 --> 00:11:45,364
a token may never look at tokens that come after it.

199
00:11:45,364 --> 00:11:50,227
In practice, those scores are set to minus infinity before the softmax, so

200
00:11:50,227 --> 00:11:53,002
their weights come out as exactly zero.

201
00:11:53,002 --> 00:11:56,777
This is the rule we met earlier, and we'll see why it matters when we get

202
00:11:56,777 --> 00:11:57,627
to training.

203
00:12:01,854 --> 00:12:05,354
In practice, all of this happens for every token at once.

204
00:12:05,354 --> 00:12:09,779
Stack the token vectors as the rows of one matrix, X.

205
00:12:09,779 --> 00:12:14,841
Multiply X by W Q, and you get a matrix Q, holding every token's query in

206
00:12:14,841 --> 00:12:15,966
its rows.

207
00:12:15,966 --> 00:12:18,945
Likewise for the keys, and the values.

208
00:12:18,945 --> 00:12:22,183
Now compare every query with every key in one go:

209
00:12:22,183 --> 00:12:27,120
that's Q times K transpose, a grid of scores with one row and one column for

210
00:12:27,120 --> 00:12:28,745
each token.

211
00:12:28,745 --> 00:12:33,608
Then divide by the square root of d k, which we'll come back to in a moment.

212
00:12:33,608 --> 00:12:37,645
Add the mask, minus infinity everywhere a token would look ahead.

213
00:12:37,645 --> 00:12:39,933
And apply softmax to each row.

214
00:12:39,933 --> 00:12:42,420
That gives the attention pattern.

215
00:12:42,420 --> 00:12:44,183
Finally, multiply by V.

216
00:12:44,183 --> 00:12:48,333
Each row of the result is one token's weighted sum of values:

217
00:12:48,333 --> 00:12:49,533
its update.

218
00:12:49,533 --> 00:12:54,208
That's the whole attention formula from the 2017 paper, and now every piece

219
00:12:54,208 --> 00:12:56,970
of it should make sense.

220
00:12:57,145 --> 00:12:59,683
So why divide by the square root of d k?

221
00:12:59,683 --> 00:13:02,545
d k is the length of each query and key:

222
00:13:02,545 --> 00:13:04,958
64 in GPT-2.

223
00:13:04,958 --> 00:13:09,820
A dot product adds up 64 products, and if those are random-ish numbers around

224
00:13:09,820 --> 00:13:14,345
one in size, the sum typically lands around plus or minus eight:

225
00:13:14,345 --> 00:13:16,879
the square root of 64.

226
00:13:16,879 --> 00:13:21,229
Feed scores that large into softmax, and nearly all the weight piles onto

227
00:13:21,229 --> 00:13:25,291
a single token, which makes training slow and unstable.

228
00:13:25,291 --> 00:13:30,629
Divide by eight, and the weights stay spread out enough to learn from.

229
00:13:30,787 --> 00:13:34,362
A single attention pattern can only capture one kind of relationship at a

230
00:13:34,362 --> 00:13:35,274
time.

231
00:13:35,274 --> 00:13:39,387
So each layer runs several attention heads in parallel, each with its own

232
00:13:39,387 --> 00:13:42,212
query, key and value matrices.

233
00:13:42,212 --> 00:13:45,724
GPT-2 small has 12 heads in every layer.

234
00:13:45,724 --> 00:13:53,079
Each works with queries and keys of 64 numbers, and 12 times 64 is 768.

235
00:13:53,079 --> 00:13:56,429
Each head produces its own update for each token.

236
00:13:56,429 --> 00:14:01,741
These are placed side by side, and multiplied by one more matrix, W O, which

237
00:14:01,741 --> 00:14:05,879
mixes them into a single update, added to the residual stream.

238
00:14:05,879 --> 00:14:12,879
With the four matrices W Q, W K, W V and W O, that's about 2.4 million parameters

239
00:14:12,879 --> 00:14:15,679
per attention layer.

240
00:14:15,820 --> 00:14:17,995
So what do real heads actually do?

241
00:14:17,995 --> 00:14:19,420
Nobody designs them:

242
00:14:19,420 --> 00:14:23,420
their matrices start out random, and whatever they end up doing emerges from

243
00:14:23,420 --> 00:14:24,445
training.

244
00:14:24,445 --> 00:14:28,020
Here are three from GPT-2.

245
00:14:28,154 --> 00:14:33,041
This one, in layer 5, sends almost all of each token's attention to the token

246
00:14:33,041 --> 00:14:34,804
immediately before it.

247
00:14:34,804 --> 00:14:38,929
It copies information about the previous token into each position.

248
00:14:38,929 --> 00:14:42,641
That sounds trivial, but it turns out to be a building block for something

249
00:14:42,641 --> 00:14:44,929
cleverer.

250
00:14:45,137 --> 00:14:46,974
Many heads look like this one:

251
00:14:46,974 --> 00:14:50,574
nearly all of their attention lands on the very first token.

252
00:14:50,574 --> 00:14:55,187
On this sentence, about two thirds of GPT-2's heads put most of their weight

253
00:14:55,187 --> 00:14:56,012
there.

254
00:14:56,012 --> 00:14:58,074
It seems to be a resting place:

255
00:14:58,074 --> 00:15:02,287
since the weights must add up to one, a head with nothing useful to contribute

256
00:15:02,287 --> 00:15:07,762
parks its attention on the first token, which contributes very little.

257
00:15:07,920 --> 00:15:10,020
The third is the most interesting.

258
00:15:10,020 --> 00:15:12,920
Feed in a short list of words, then repeat it.

259
00:15:12,920 --> 00:15:16,870
On the second pass, at each word, this head looks back at the word that came

260
00:15:16,870 --> 00:15:19,820
right after that same word's first appearance.

261
00:15:19,820 --> 00:15:24,170
When it reaches river the second time, it looks at lamp, the word that followed

262
00:15:24,170 --> 00:15:25,670
river last time.

263
00:15:25,670 --> 00:15:28,029
It's called an induction head.

264
00:15:28,029 --> 00:15:29,666
Its rule amounts to:

265
00:15:29,666 --> 00:15:33,729
if this word appeared before, attend to whatever came after it, and predict

266
00:15:33,729 --> 00:15:34,904
that again.

267
00:15:34,904 --> 00:15:38,916
It relies on a previous-token head from an earlier layer, like the first one

268
00:15:38,916 --> 00:15:43,104
we saw, to know what preceded each word.

269
00:15:43,279 --> 00:15:46,516
Watch what that does to GPT-2's predictions.

270
00:15:46,516 --> 00:15:50,891
Here's a random list of 20 words, followed by the same list again.

271
00:15:50,891 --> 00:15:55,616
For each word, the bar shows the probability GPT-2 gave it, just before it

272
00:15:55,616 --> 00:15:56,641
appeared.

273
00:15:56,641 --> 00:16:01,662
The first time through, the words are random, so every bar is tiny.

274
00:16:01,662 --> 00:16:04,912
The second time, it predicts almost every word.

275
00:16:04,912 --> 00:16:09,324
It has picked up a pattern from its own context, something it was never explicitly

276
00:16:09,324 --> 00:16:10,262
taught.

277
00:16:10,262 --> 00:16:14,437
This is a simple form of what's called in-context learning, and induction

278
00:16:14,437 --> 00:16:17,187
heads seem to be one of its basic mechanisms.

279
00:16:21,390 --> 00:16:23,815
There's a gap in what we've built so far.

280
00:16:23,815 --> 00:16:26,503
Nothing in attention knows where a token is:

281
00:16:26,503 --> 00:16:30,928
queries, keys and values depend only on each token's vector.

282
00:16:30,928 --> 00:16:34,953
Take the sentence 'dog bites man', and look at it from the point of view of

283
00:16:34,953 --> 00:16:37,065
the last token, the period.

284
00:16:37,065 --> 00:16:41,265
It sees three words, and takes a weighted sum of their values.

285
00:16:41,265 --> 00:16:43,415
Now swap dog and man.

286
00:16:43,415 --> 00:16:47,853
The period sees exactly the same three words, with the same values, and a

287
00:16:47,853 --> 00:16:50,415
weighted sum doesn't care about order.

288
00:16:50,415 --> 00:16:54,865
So at least in the first layer, it computes exactly the same thing, even though

289
00:16:54,865 --> 00:16:58,790
the sentences mean very different things.

290
00:16:58,890 --> 00:16:59,765
The fix:

291
00:16:59,765 --> 00:17:03,978
give each position its own vector, and add it to the token's vector before

292
00:17:03,978 --> 00:17:05,490
the first layer.

293
00:17:05,490 --> 00:17:09,465
Now the same word at a different position starts out as a slightly different

294
00:17:09,465 --> 00:17:11,665
vector.

295
00:17:11,840 --> 00:17:16,378
In GPT-2, these position vectors are learned during training, just like the

296
00:17:16,378 --> 00:17:17,853
token embeddings:

297
00:17:17,853 --> 00:17:22,140
one for each of the 1,024 positions the model can handle.

298
00:17:22,140 --> 00:17:26,478
Here they are as a heat map, with position running down, and some of the dimensions

299
00:17:26,478 --> 00:17:27,915
running across.

300
00:17:27,915 --> 00:17:31,898
Training discovered smooth, wave-like patterns.

301
00:17:31,898 --> 00:17:37,923
Now project all 1,024 position vectors onto their three most important directions,

302
00:17:37,923 --> 00:17:40,423
and they trace out a smooth spiral.

303
00:17:40,423 --> 00:17:44,486
Neighboring positions get similar vectors, so the model can tell both where

304
00:17:44,486 --> 00:17:49,973
a token is, and roughly how far apart two tokens are.

305
00:17:52,198 --> 00:17:56,886
Many newer models, including Meta's Llama family, use a different trick, called

306
00:17:56,886 --> 00:17:59,011
rotary position embeddings.

307
00:17:59,011 --> 00:18:03,248
Instead of adding a position vector, they rotate the queries and keys.

308
00:18:03,248 --> 00:18:07,361
Split each query and key into pairs of numbers, and think of each pair as

309
00:18:07,361 --> 00:18:09,457
an arrow in a plane.

310
00:18:09,457 --> 00:18:14,519
A query at position m is rotated by m times some angle, and a key at position

311
00:18:14,519 --> 00:18:16,994
n by n times that angle.

312
00:18:16,994 --> 00:18:21,357
In the dot product, only the angle between the two arrows matters, and that

313
00:18:21,357 --> 00:18:24,240
depends only on m minus n.

314
00:18:24,240 --> 00:18:27,503
So slide both tokens along by the same amount:

315
00:18:27,503 --> 00:18:31,565
both arrows turn together, and the dot product doesn't change.

316
00:18:31,565 --> 00:18:36,215
Attention becomes sensitive to how far apart two tokens are, which is usually

317
00:18:36,215 --> 00:18:41,740
what matters in language, rather than to their absolute positions.

318
00:18:41,907 --> 00:18:45,457
Different pairs of numbers rotate at different speeds, like the hands of a

319
00:18:45,457 --> 00:18:46,382
clock.

320
00:18:46,382 --> 00:18:49,282
Fast pairs distinguish nearby positions;

321
00:18:49,282 --> 00:18:52,873
slow pairs keep track of long distances.

322
00:18:52,873 --> 00:18:57,448
So now each vector knows what its token is, where it sits, and, through attention,

323
00:18:57,448 --> 00:18:58,798
what's around it.

324
00:18:58,798 --> 00:18:59,473
Next:

325
00:18:59,473 --> 00:19:01,223
the other half of every layer.

326
00:19:05,503 --> 00:19:09,141
After attention comes the second kind of step in every layer:

327
00:19:09,141 --> 00:19:12,341
a multilayer perceptron, or MLP.

328
00:19:12,341 --> 00:19:16,066
Unlike attention, it works on each position separately.

329
00:19:16,066 --> 00:19:18,378
The vectors don't talk to each other here.

330
00:19:18,378 --> 00:19:24,478
And every position goes through the very same MLP, with the same weights.

331
00:19:24,612 --> 00:19:26,937
An MLP does three things.

332
00:19:26,937 --> 00:19:33,349
First, it multiplies the vector by a big matrix, expanding it from 768 numbers

333
00:19:33,349 --> 00:19:35,512
to 3,072.

334
00:19:35,512 --> 00:19:40,149
Then it applies a simple nonlinear function to each of those numbers.

335
00:19:40,149 --> 00:19:45,537
Then a second matrix brings it back down to 768 numbers, and the result is

336
00:19:45,537 --> 00:19:48,078
added to the residual stream.

337
00:19:48,078 --> 00:19:50,078
Here's a useful way to read it.

338
00:19:50,078 --> 00:19:51,728
Look at a single neuron.

339
00:19:51,728 --> 00:19:55,703
Its incoming weights, one row of the first matrix, form a direction in the

340
00:19:55,703 --> 00:19:56,716
space.

341
00:19:56,716 --> 00:19:59,266
Taking the dot product with it asks a question:

342
00:19:59,266 --> 00:20:02,091
how much does this token's vector point that way?

343
00:20:02,091 --> 00:20:05,353
Add a bias, and that's the neuron's input.

344
00:20:05,353 --> 00:20:10,141
The nonlinearity, called GELU, squashes negative inputs to nearly zero, and

345
00:20:10,141 --> 00:20:12,241
passes positive ones through.

346
00:20:12,241 --> 00:20:15,812
So each neuron is either quiet, or firing.

347
00:20:15,812 --> 00:20:20,637
And when neuron i fires, its outgoing weights, one column of the second matrix,

348
00:20:20,637 --> 00:20:24,612
are added to the vector, scaled by how strongly it fired.

349
00:20:24,612 --> 00:20:26,899
So the whole MLP is a sum:

350
00:20:26,899 --> 00:20:32,587
a long list of directions, each one switched on by its own question.

351
00:20:32,770 --> 00:20:34,770
So, in a cartoon version:

352
00:20:34,770 --> 00:20:38,820
by the time our prompt reaches some layer, the vector at the word 'of' might

353
00:20:38,820 --> 00:20:42,945
encode that the text mentions the Eiffel Tower, and that a city name should

354
00:20:42,945 --> 00:20:44,245
come next.

355
00:20:44,245 --> 00:20:48,683
A neuron whose question matches that combination fires, and writes a direction

356
00:20:48,683 --> 00:20:51,203
meaning Paris into the vector.

357
00:20:51,203 --> 00:20:53,041
Real models are messier:

358
00:20:53,041 --> 00:20:57,478
each fact seems to be spread across many neurons, and each neuron takes part

359
00:20:57,478 --> 00:20:59,128
in many different things.

360
00:20:59,128 --> 00:21:03,541
But experiments that edit or switch off parts of these layers suggest that

361
00:21:03,541 --> 00:21:07,853
the MLPs store much of what a model knows.

362
00:21:08,012 --> 00:21:13,074
Here are the real neurons of one of GPT-2's MLP layers, at the last token

363
00:21:13,074 --> 00:21:14,224
of our prompt.

364
00:21:14,224 --> 00:21:19,049
Out of 3,072, only about one in thirty fires strongly.

365
00:21:19,049 --> 00:21:21,720
The rest stay close to zero.

366
00:21:21,720 --> 00:21:26,158
The two matrices hold about 4.7 million parameters per layer:

367
00:21:26,158 --> 00:21:28,408
twice as many as attention.

368
00:21:28,408 --> 00:21:34,245
In large models, that makes the MLPs home to most of all the weights.

369
00:21:34,378 --> 00:21:35,666
Here's a puzzle.

370
00:21:35,666 --> 00:21:42,903
A space with 768 dimensions has room for only 768 directions that are exactly

371
00:21:42,903 --> 00:21:44,916
perpendicular to each other.

372
00:21:44,916 --> 00:21:50,353
Yet a model seems to know about far more than 768 different things.

373
00:21:50,353 --> 00:21:54,466
Part of the answer is a strange fact about high-dimensional space.

374
00:21:54,466 --> 00:21:58,666
Pick random directions in three dimensions, and the angles between them are

375
00:21:58,666 --> 00:22:00,191
all over the place.

376
00:22:00,191 --> 00:22:04,216
In 30 dimensions, they bunch up around ninety degrees.

377
00:22:04,216 --> 00:22:10,737
In 768 dimensions, almost every pair is within a few degrees of perpendicular.

378
00:22:10,737 --> 00:22:15,062
In fact, the number of directions you can fit that are all nearly perpendicular

379
00:22:15,062 --> 00:22:18,112
grows exponentially with the dimension.

380
00:22:18,112 --> 00:22:22,187
Models appear to take advantage of this, packing many more features than they

381
00:22:22,187 --> 00:22:26,512
have dimensions into the same space, each in its own nearly perpendicular

382
00:22:26,512 --> 00:22:27,699
direction.

383
00:22:27,699 --> 00:22:31,862
Researchers call this superposition, and it's one reason the insides of these

384
00:22:31,862 --> 00:22:33,887
models are so hard to read.

385
00:22:38,166 --> 00:22:41,929
Let's assemble one complete layer, called a transformer block.

386
00:22:41,929 --> 00:22:45,229
Picture the residual stream as a line running upward.

387
00:22:45,229 --> 00:22:49,791
The attention step branches off, reads the current vectors, and adds its result

388
00:22:49,791 --> 00:22:50,891
back in.

389
00:22:50,891 --> 00:22:53,054
Then the MLP does the same:

390
00:22:53,054 --> 00:22:56,366
it reads, and adds its result back in.

391
00:22:56,366 --> 00:22:58,091
One detail I've skipped:

392
00:22:58,091 --> 00:23:02,741
before each branch reads the stream, the vector passes through a layer norm.

393
00:23:02,741 --> 00:23:07,041
It shifts each vector so that its numbers average to zero, rescales them to

394
00:23:07,041 --> 00:23:11,104
a standard size, then applies a learned scale and shift.

395
00:23:11,104 --> 00:23:15,441
This keeps the numbers in a healthy range as they pass through dozens of layers,

396
00:23:15,441 --> 00:23:19,066
which makes training much more stable.

397
00:23:19,208 --> 00:23:21,571
A useful summary of the two halves:

398
00:23:21,571 --> 00:23:24,796
attention moves information between positions.

399
00:23:24,796 --> 00:23:30,208
The MLP processes the information sitting at each position.

400
00:23:30,341 --> 00:23:33,266
Then stack these blocks, one on top of another.

401
00:23:33,266 --> 00:23:35,766
GPT-2 small has 12.

402
00:23:35,766 --> 00:23:38,104
GPT-3 has 96.

403
00:23:38,104 --> 00:23:42,483
Embeddings go in at the bottom, and the prediction comes out at the top.

404
00:23:42,483 --> 00:23:46,458
Now we can count every single parameter in GPT-2 small.

405
00:23:46,458 --> 00:23:48,196
The token embedding table:

406
00:23:48,196 --> 00:23:55,096
50,257 tokens times 768 numbers, about 38.6 million.

407
00:23:55,096 --> 00:23:56,308
Position embeddings:

408
00:23:56,308 --> 00:23:57,671
under a million.

409
00:23:57,671 --> 00:23:59,196
Each attention layer:

410
00:23:59,196 --> 00:24:02,271
about 2.4 million, each MLP:

411
00:24:02,271 --> 00:24:07,000
about 4.7 million, plus a few thousand for the layer norms.

412
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:15,287
Multiply the per-block numbers by 12, add everything up, and you get 124,439,808

413
00:24:15,287 --> 00:24:16,550
parameters:

414
00:24:16,550 --> 00:24:20,500
the 124 million in GPT-2 small's name.

415
00:24:20,500 --> 00:24:25,037
Notice that almost a third of this small model is just the embedding table.

416
00:24:25,037 --> 00:24:27,500
In bigger models, the blocks dominate.

417
00:24:31,715 --> 00:24:35,577
At the top of the stack, take the vector at the last position, the one for

418
00:24:35,577 --> 00:24:36,315
'of'.

419
00:24:36,315 --> 00:24:40,027
By now, it has absorbed information from the whole prompt.

420
00:24:40,027 --> 00:24:44,715
After one last layer norm, it's time to turn it into a prediction.

421
00:24:44,715 --> 00:24:49,015
The vector is compared, by dot product, with a vector for every token in the

422
00:24:49,015 --> 00:24:50,440
vocabulary.

423
00:24:50,440 --> 00:24:54,552
GPT-2 simply reuses its embedding table for this.

424
00:24:54,552 --> 00:25:01,906
The result is 50,257 scores, called logits, one for each possible next token.

425
00:25:01,906 --> 00:25:06,794
Here, GPT-2's raw logits all happen to sit around minus 95.

426
00:25:06,794 --> 00:25:08,019
That doesn't matter:

427
00:25:08,019 --> 00:25:12,206
adding the same number to every score doesn't change softmax, so only the

428
00:25:12,206 --> 00:25:13,744
differences count.

429
00:25:13,744 --> 00:25:18,269
Softmax turns them into probabilities, and Paris comes out on top, at about

430
00:25:18,269 --> 00:25:19,940
six percent.

431
00:25:19,940 --> 00:25:23,827
In fact, every position in the sequence makes a prediction like this, for

432
00:25:23,827 --> 00:25:25,702
the token that follows it.

433
00:25:25,702 --> 00:25:28,515
To generate text we only need the last one;

434
00:25:28,515 --> 00:25:33,165
during training, as we'll see, all of them are used.

435
00:25:33,306 --> 00:25:36,206
Here's a fun trick, called the logit lens.

436
00:25:36,206 --> 00:25:40,206
Nothing stops us from applying this final step early, to the residual stream

437
00:25:40,206 --> 00:25:42,306
after any layer, and asking:

438
00:25:42,306 --> 00:25:44,519
what would the model predict right now?

439
00:25:44,519 --> 00:25:49,819
After the embedding alone, Paris sits at rank 22,947.

440
00:25:49,819 --> 00:25:53,031
The early layers mostly guess 'the'.

441
00:25:53,031 --> 00:25:55,394
Around the middle, place names start to appear:

442
00:25:55,394 --> 00:25:56,769
England, Rome.

443
00:25:56,769 --> 00:25:59,319
After layer 9, Paris is 13th;

444
00:25:59,319 --> 00:26:01,106
after layer 10, second;

445
00:26:01,106 --> 00:26:03,144
after layer 11, first.

446
00:26:03,144 --> 00:26:07,756
You can watch the answer take shape, layer by layer.

447
00:26:07,906 --> 00:26:11,969
To generate text, we have to pick a token from these probabilities.

448
00:26:11,969 --> 00:26:16,194
Here's GPT-2's distribution after a more open-ended prompt:

449
00:26:16,194 --> 00:26:18,494
once upon a time, there was a...

450
00:26:18,494 --> 00:26:22,290
Always picking the top choice is called greedy decoding.

451
00:26:22,290 --> 00:26:25,102
But greedy decoding tends to get stuck in loops:

452
00:26:25,102 --> 00:26:27,952
a man who was a man of great wealth and power.

453
00:26:27,952 --> 00:26:30,377
He was a man of great wealth and power.

454
00:26:30,377 --> 00:26:34,015
He was a man of great wealth and power...

455
00:26:34,173 --> 00:26:39,023
So instead, we usually sample at random, in proportion to the probabilities,

456
00:26:39,023 --> 00:26:43,235
after first dividing every logit by a number called the temperature.

457
00:26:43,235 --> 00:26:47,785
A temperature below one sharpens the distribution toward the top choices.

458
00:26:47,785 --> 00:26:53,848
Above one, it flattens it out, giving unlikely tokens more of a chance.

459
00:26:54,006 --> 00:26:56,781
Here are real samples from GPT-2.

460
00:26:56,781 --> 00:27:01,431
At a temperature of 0.7, it writes about a bright star in the sky.

461
00:27:01,431 --> 00:27:03,981
At 1, it gets more surprising.

462
00:27:03,981 --> 00:27:09,106
At 1.6, the long tail of unlikely tokens takes over, and it dissolves into

463
00:27:09,106 --> 00:27:10,281
word salad.

464
00:27:10,281 --> 00:27:15,156
Many applications use temperatures somewhere between about 0.7 and 1.

465
00:27:19,403 --> 00:27:23,890
Every number we've seen so far, the embeddings, the attention matrices, the

466
00:27:23,890 --> 00:27:29,040
MLP weights, all 124 million of them, starts out random.

467
00:27:29,040 --> 00:27:33,040
A freshly initialized model has no idea what comes next:

468
00:27:33,040 --> 00:27:37,440
its predictions are spread almost evenly, about one in fifty thousand for

469
00:27:37,440 --> 00:27:40,028
every token.

470
00:27:40,170 --> 00:27:42,507
To improve, the model needs a score.

471
00:27:42,507 --> 00:27:46,120
Take any text, and at each position, look at the probability the model gave

472
00:27:46,120 --> 00:27:48,370
to the token that actually came next.

473
00:27:48,370 --> 00:27:51,420
The loss is minus the logarithm of that probability.

474
00:27:51,420 --> 00:27:54,432
If the model was certain and right, the loss is zero.

475
00:27:54,432 --> 00:27:58,232
For our Paris, at six percent, it's 2.75.

476
00:27:58,232 --> 00:28:01,782
A model that guesses uniformly scores about 10.8.

477
00:28:01,782 --> 00:28:05,470
Averaged over lots of text, this one number is what training tries to make

478
00:28:05,470 --> 00:28:07,620
small.

479
00:28:07,745 --> 00:28:11,607
And one piece of text gives a prediction at every position at once.

480
00:28:11,607 --> 00:28:15,082
Here are GPT-2's real losses on our sentence.

481
00:28:15,082 --> 00:28:18,270
After 'The', the token E was a big surprise.

482
00:28:18,270 --> 00:28:22,507
But once it has seen E and iff, the model is certain that el comes next, and

483
00:28:22,507 --> 00:28:23,682
then Tower.

484
00:28:23,682 --> 00:28:26,703
Paris costs 2.75.

485
00:28:26,703 --> 00:28:29,390
This is why the causal mask matters.

486
00:28:29,390 --> 00:28:34,078
Each position's prediction may only use the tokens before it, so all of these

487
00:28:34,078 --> 00:28:38,678
predictions can be trained at the same time, in parallel, without any of them

488
00:28:38,678 --> 00:28:41,978
getting to see its own answer.

489
00:28:42,136 --> 00:28:45,711
So how do you improve 124 million numbers at once?

490
00:28:45,711 --> 00:28:48,061
Measure the loss on a batch of text.

491
00:28:48,061 --> 00:28:51,986
For every parameter, calculus tells you how the loss would change if you nudged

492
00:28:51,986 --> 00:28:53,036
it slightly;

493
00:28:53,036 --> 00:28:55,799
together, these slopes make up the gradient.

494
00:28:55,799 --> 00:29:00,824
Move every parameter a small step downhill, and repeat, on fresh text, millions

495
00:29:00,824 --> 00:29:01,899
of times.

496
00:29:01,899 --> 00:29:04,070
This is gradient descent.

497
00:29:04,070 --> 00:29:09,432
Computing 124 million slopes sounds expensive, but there's a trick:

498
00:29:09,432 --> 00:29:13,920
apply the chain rule backwards through the layers, reusing work as you go,

499
00:29:13,920 --> 00:29:18,182
and you get all of them for roughly the cost of a couple more forward passes.

500
00:29:18,182 --> 00:29:22,370
That algorithm is called backpropagation.

501
00:29:22,503 --> 00:29:26,653
To see learning happen, I trained a tiny transformer from scratch.

502
00:29:26,653 --> 00:29:32,128
It's the same design as GPT-2, but with just 0.8 million parameters, four

503
00:29:32,128 --> 00:29:36,778
layers, and single characters as tokens, trained on about a million characters

504
00:29:36,778 --> 00:29:38,153
of Shakespeare.

505
00:29:38,153 --> 00:29:40,203
Here's its real loss curve.

506
00:29:40,203 --> 00:29:44,111
It starts at the loss of random guessing, and falls fast.

507
00:29:44,111 --> 00:29:47,736
At step zero, it produces random characters.

508
00:29:47,736 --> 00:29:52,061
After a hundred steps, it has learned which letters are common, and that text

509
00:29:52,061 --> 00:29:55,245
comes in chunks separated by spaces.

510
00:29:55,245 --> 00:29:59,770
By step 300, there are short real words, line breaks, and made-up names in

511
00:29:59,770 --> 00:30:04,778
capitals, followed by a colon, just like the speaker names in a play.

512
00:30:04,778 --> 00:30:06,315
By step 1,000:

513
00:30:06,315 --> 00:30:10,711
real words, real names, like Gloucester, and the rhythm of dialogue.

514
00:30:10,711 --> 00:30:15,436
And after 4,000 steps, about a quarter of an hour on four ordinary processor

515
00:30:15,436 --> 00:30:17,461
cores, it writes this.

516
00:30:17,461 --> 00:30:22,061
It's not great Shakespeare, but nobody told it about spelling, words, names,

517
00:30:22,061 --> 00:30:23,474
or line breaks.

518
00:30:23,474 --> 00:30:26,795
All of it came from predicting the next character.

519
00:30:26,795 --> 00:30:31,332
GPT-2 learned in exactly the same way, just bigger:

520
00:30:31,332 --> 00:30:35,370
predicting the next token across about 40 gigabytes of text from the web.

521
00:30:39,665 --> 00:30:42,678
Everything so far has been GPT-2 small.

522
00:30:42,678 --> 00:30:47,478
OpenAI actually released four sizes of GPT-2, all with the same design, just

523
00:30:47,478 --> 00:30:48,853
wider and deeper.

524
00:30:48,853 --> 00:30:50,590
Let's give each of them our prompt.

525
00:30:50,590 --> 00:30:53,190
The smallest gives Paris six percent.

526
00:30:53,190 --> 00:30:54,115
The next one up:

527
00:30:54,115 --> 00:30:55,303
69.

528
00:30:55,303 --> 00:30:56,328
The one after that:

529
00:30:56,328 --> 00:30:59,940
60, so it's not perfectly smooth for any single prompt.

530
00:30:59,940 --> 00:31:04,015
And the largest, with one and a half billion parameters, puts 93 percent on

531
00:31:04,015 --> 00:31:06,240
Paris.

532
00:31:06,365 --> 00:31:11,340
In 2020, researchers at OpenAI measured this systematically, training language

533
00:31:11,340 --> 00:31:14,203
models across a huge range of sizes.

534
00:31:14,203 --> 00:31:16,828
The loss followed a smooth power law.

535
00:31:16,828 --> 00:31:21,040
On a log-log plot, that's a straight line, and it held over many orders of

536
00:31:21,040 --> 00:31:22,028
magnitude:

537
00:31:22,028 --> 00:31:26,599
in model size, in the amount of training data, and in compute.

538
00:31:26,599 --> 00:31:30,861
Two years later, DeepMind's Chinchilla study refined the recipe:

539
00:31:30,861 --> 00:31:35,486
for a fixed compute budget, model size and training data should grow together,

540
00:31:35,486 --> 00:31:39,336
at roughly twenty tokens of training text for every parameter.

541
00:31:39,336 --> 00:31:43,674
That's how you get the most out of each unit of compute.

542
00:31:43,807 --> 00:31:47,782
Here's how much models have grown, with area showing parameter count.

543
00:31:47,782 --> 00:31:50,445
Our GPT-2 small is this speck.

544
00:31:50,445 --> 00:31:52,320
The largest GPT-2:

545
00:31:52,320 --> 00:31:54,757
1.5 billion parameters.

546
00:31:54,757 --> 00:31:57,107
GPT-3, in 2020:

547
00:31:57,107 --> 00:32:04,982
175 billion parameters, 96 layers, vectors of 12,288 numbers, trained on 300

548
00:32:04,982 --> 00:32:06,807
billion tokens.

549
00:32:06,807 --> 00:32:13,282
Meta's Llama 3.1, in 2024, one of the largest models whose details are public:

550
00:32:13,282 --> 00:32:20,457
405 billion parameters, 126 layers, trained on about 15 trillion tokens.

551
00:32:20,457 --> 00:32:25,182
Most frontier labs no longer publish these numbers.

552
00:32:25,357 --> 00:32:27,645
A handy rule of thumb for the cost:

553
00:32:27,645 --> 00:32:31,782
training takes about six arithmetic operations per parameter, per token of

554
00:32:31,782 --> 00:32:33,045
training text:

555
00:32:33,045 --> 00:32:36,732
two for the forward pass, and four more for backpropagation.

556
00:32:36,732 --> 00:32:41,645
For GPT-3, that's about 3 times 10 to the 23 operations.

557
00:32:41,645 --> 00:32:49,132
For Llama 3.1, about 4 times 10 to the 25, which matches what Meta reported.

558
00:32:49,315 --> 00:32:52,178
Modern models also refine many details:

559
00:32:52,178 --> 00:32:58,190
rotary positions, which we saw, simpler normalization, gated MLPs, attention

560
00:32:58,190 --> 00:33:03,090
heads that share keys and values to save memory, and mixture-of-experts layers,

561
00:33:03,090 --> 00:33:07,565
where each token is routed to just a few of many MLPs, so a model can hold

562
00:33:07,565 --> 00:33:10,965
far more parameters than it uses for any one token.

563
00:33:10,965 --> 00:33:17,715
And context windows have grown from GPT-2's 1,024 tokens to hundreds of thousands.

564
00:33:17,715 --> 00:33:20,390
But the blueprint is the one we've just walked through.

565
00:33:24,688 --> 00:33:29,113
A model trained only to predict text from the internet is not an assistant.

566
00:33:29,113 --> 00:33:30,913
It's a document completer.

567
00:33:30,913 --> 00:33:35,938
Ask the largest GPT-2 to write a short poem about the ocean, and it continues

568
00:33:35,938 --> 00:33:39,326
the document the way such documents often continue:

569
00:33:39,326 --> 00:33:41,363
by repeating the instruction.

570
00:33:41,363 --> 00:33:43,622
Again, and again.

571
00:33:43,622 --> 00:33:47,834
Ask it a question, and it answers, then invents the next question, as if it

572
00:33:47,834 --> 00:33:50,572
were writing a quiz.

573
00:33:50,697 --> 00:33:54,959
A chatbot is the same machine, completing a different kind of document:

574
00:33:54,959 --> 00:33:58,584
a transcript, with special tokens marking who's speaking.

575
00:33:58,584 --> 00:34:00,984
Your message goes in the user's slot.

576
00:34:00,984 --> 00:34:05,259
Then the model predicts what the assistant says, one token at a time, until

577
00:34:05,259 --> 00:34:07,584
it predicts a special token that means:

578
00:34:07,584 --> 00:34:10,147
end of turn.

579
00:34:10,297 --> 00:34:14,497
Turning a document completer into an assistant takes extra training.

580
00:34:14,497 --> 00:34:16,659
First comes everything we've seen:

581
00:34:16,659 --> 00:34:20,622
pretraining on enormous amounts of text, which is where the language and the

582
00:34:20,622 --> 00:34:22,184
knowledge come from.

583
00:34:22,184 --> 00:34:27,309
Then supervised fine-tuning, on example conversations written by people, with

584
00:34:27,309 --> 00:34:32,297
the very same next-token loss, now applied to the assistant's replies.

585
00:34:32,297 --> 00:34:34,409
Then, learning from feedback:

586
00:34:34,409 --> 00:34:38,972
people compare pairs of answers, a separate reward model learns to predict

587
00:34:38,972 --> 00:34:43,097
which ones they prefer, and the language model is tuned with reinforcement

588
00:34:43,097 --> 00:34:46,597
learning to produce answers that score well.

589
00:34:46,597 --> 00:34:48,934
This matters more than you might think.

590
00:34:48,934 --> 00:34:55,447
In OpenAI's 2022 InstructGPT paper, people preferred the answers of a 1.3

591
00:34:55,447 --> 00:35:00,747
billion parameter model trained this way over those of the original GPT-3,

592
00:35:00,747 --> 00:35:04,572
which is more than a hundred times larger.

593
00:35:04,755 --> 00:35:09,043
More recently, models are also trained with reinforcement learning on problems

594
00:35:09,043 --> 00:35:13,530
whose answers can be checked automatically, like math and programming.

595
00:35:13,530 --> 00:35:17,043
Models trained this way learn to write out long chains of reasoning before

596
00:35:17,043 --> 00:35:20,330
they answer, and get much better at hard problems.

597
00:35:20,330 --> 00:35:22,580
But underneath it's still the same loop:

598
00:35:22,580 --> 00:35:25,938
predict the next token, append it, repeat.

599
00:35:25,938 --> 00:35:28,851
That also explains a familiar failure.

600
00:35:28,851 --> 00:35:33,163
The model always produces a plausible-sounding continuation, whether or not

601
00:35:33,163 --> 00:35:34,951
it happens to be true.

602
00:35:34,951 --> 00:35:38,963
So it can state something false with complete fluency, which is why checking

603
00:35:38,963 --> 00:35:44,088
claims, and grounding models in reliable sources, is an active area of work.

604
00:35:45,750 --> 00:35:47,238
Let's put it all together.

605
00:35:47,238 --> 00:35:49,463
Text is chopped into tokens.

606
00:35:49,463 --> 00:35:53,450
Each token becomes a vector, plus a vector for its position.

607
00:35:53,450 --> 00:35:57,350
Those vectors flow up through a stack of blocks, where attention lets them

608
00:35:57,350 --> 00:36:02,375
share information, the MLPs process what each one holds, and every step adds

609
00:36:02,375 --> 00:36:04,367
to the residual stream.

610
00:36:04,367 --> 00:36:08,742
At the top, the last vector is compared with every token in the vocabulary,

611
00:36:08,742 --> 00:36:12,067
and softmax turns the scores into probabilities.

612
00:36:12,067 --> 00:36:17,592
One token is chosen, appended, and the whole thing runs again.

613
00:36:18,225 --> 00:36:20,850
Here's the part I find most remarkable.

614
00:36:20,850 --> 00:36:25,400
None of what we found inside GPT-2, the head that links bank to river, the

615
00:36:25,400 --> 00:36:30,663
induction heads, Paris emerging layer by layer, was designed by anyone.

616
00:36:30,663 --> 00:36:33,538
The architecture fits in a few pages of code.

617
00:36:33,538 --> 00:36:36,825
Everything else was learned, from one simple objective:

618
00:36:36,825 --> 00:36:39,059
predict the next token.

619
00:36:39,059 --> 00:36:43,259
Working out, in detail, what all those billions of learned numbers are actually

620
00:36:43,259 --> 00:36:49,746
doing is still an open scientific problem, and a very active one, called interpretability.

621
00:36:49,746 --> 00:36:53,159
You now know the machine they live in.

622
00:36:53,342 --> 00:36:54,517
Thanks for watching.
